\f1\fs22  Gerald Hardman (\'91Gerry\'92 at home, and \'91Very\'92 among agency colleagues) proceeded with haste from the hover-copt, across the pad, adjusting his mask and pulling his mackintosh tighter about himself. The morning\'92s weather was squally, and was whipping up peaks of the Layer, which occasionally rose and wafted over the  lower sprouts of hover-pads. Gerry held his breath until he got inside.\
As usual, the main foyer of Ulyssia Police Incorporated was as reverently quiet as a cathedral. Busy dark suits, imposing uniforms, and sensibly cut skirts whispered; polished shoes scuffed and clacked over the ever-changing hologram of the CEO in the floor.\
In the holy cavernous space, Gerry maintained a practiced veneer of hushed awe until he passed the security turnstyles, the detect-everything detectors, and reached the sullen oppressive elevator hall and took a lift depressingly down. Even as Gerry progressed briskly through middle administrative management (as engineered by his PAPER masters and mistresses) his offices\'97while locating ever nearer a prestigious window\'97never rose above the Layer. His present assignment, while a prestigious position, landed him a corner office with panoramic views of septic yellow-white and fetid hospital-green swirls of corrosive agents which had, in less than six months tarnished, etched and corroded the external glass so badly, Gerry could be looking at a crazed necrotic mirror He almost felt sorry for the Cloudborne maintenance team who must soon don Hazmat suits to replace the external glazing. He 
\f2\i would 
\f1\i0 feel sorry for them, except they were Cloudborne who, by their own indifference to their station and demonstrable lack effort in changing it, deserved none of his attention at all. \
Gerry tossed his tablet on his desk, turned out the lights, stood and squashed his nose against a window. It made little difference; the view never changed.\
His tablet binged.\
\'91Excuse me,\'92 his assistant constable said.\
\'91What?\'92 With his nose still pressed against the glass, he sounded sinus-blocked.\
\'91You have a meeting in the Commissioner\'92s office.\'92\
\'91When?\'92\
\'91Immediately.\'92\
\
\'91You can go right in,\'92 the Commissioner\'92s assistant constable said in a tone so warm, Gerry was instantly on guard. Perhaps his activities there had finally been uncovered. Gerry reluctantly pushed at the great heavy door so reluctantly, it failed to budge until he put more shoulder into locomoting it. Once inside, he was immediately met with \'85\
\'85blindness. Morning sunlight peeled in from the Commissioner\'92s multi-storey windows and made Gerry squint so hard, he could make neither whether anyone was sitting in the great officious chair behind the great officious desk, nor the identity of the square-headed silhouette standing alongside in a coronet of blaring rainbow light.\
\'91Come in,\'92 said a voice\'97not the Commissioner\'92s, yet somehow familiar.\
Gerry warily trod the many metres between door and desk, realising the standing shadow had PAPER written all over it. Was it his saviour, or a counter-signatory on the warrant of his execution?\
A figure emerged from the glare-ringed shadow of the chair and stood up. \'91Have a seat,\'92 the figure said and gestured at the Commissioner\'92s seat.\
